{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And he came into Rachel's tent.</b> Rashi explains: when he went out of Leah's tent, he turned and came into Rachel's tent before searching the tent of the maidservants. What compelled Rashi to say that he searched the maidservants' tent last? Rather, because it says <b>and he did not find</b> in connection with the tent of the two maidservants, this teaches us that he searched their tent last. And one must rescribe the verse: 'and he came into Jacob's tent and into Leah's tent, and he went out of Leah's tent and came into Rachel's tent and into the tent of the two maidservants and did not find.' And Rachel's tent was in the middle, because she is the mainstay of the household and Jacob was there constantly. And Leah's tent was on this side and the tent of the two maidservants on this side, and all of them opened into Rachel's tent and into the public domain. And one may say according to the plain reading of the verse: Leah's tent was in the middle, and Rachel's tent on this side, and the maidservants' tent on this side; and Leah's and Rachel's tents opened into the public domain, but the maidservants' tent did not open into the public domain, so that Jacob would not be able to enter it without Rachel's or Leah's knowledge. And therefore when he went out of the maidservants' tent he entered Leah's tent, and when he went out of Leah's tent he came into Rachel's tent."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:57.519467075Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}